TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction, Executive Summary"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, Table 4"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit (Manoeuvres)"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications, Table 5"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, 2.4, Appendix A (STK 11)"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Figures 1, 3, 4; Appendix A"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Contents, Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"X-band link budget not performed; 50 Mbit/s downlink and ground-station sizing are asserted rather than demonstrated."},{"where":"Table 6","issue":"Imaging plan assumes only 60,000 km² per day (one 1,000 km strip), far below the satellite's actual capability; revisit/mosaic logic is therefore conservative but internally inconsistent with a pushbroom imager."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","issue":"Revisit calculation (2,698 km / 560 km ≈ 5 days) ignores latitude convergence and assumes single-pass access; real coverage at 18°S uses ~2,570 km track spacing, giving ~4.6 days - acceptable but not stated."},{"where":"2.2 Launch","issue":"Vega C status ('has not yet flown, first flight planned 2021') is accurate for 2020 but should be flagged as time-dependent; rideshare injection accuracy and exact release altitude are not specified."},{"where":"Figure 2","issue":"Inclination-vs-altitude curve has no stated source or generation method (STK/MATLAB) and no data file reference."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant is a VLBI/geodesy facility; addition of S/X-band hardware, licensing and cost are assumed rather than designed, with no elevation mask used in the access report."}],"overall":"A strong, technically sound and well-evidenced preliminary design: launch, SSO orbit, elements, manoeuvres, ground segment, duration and drag-sail EOL are all covered with verified calculations, STK outputs and files. Main gaps are the missing X-band link budget, an over-conservative imaging plan, and a few vague references.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Mission, need, spacecraft count and users (operator AIMS; customers GBRMPA, CSIRO, BoM) all covered with reasoning and citations [1]-[6]. Rationale for direct bleaching detection is well argued. Minor: source [4] is vaguely cited (Nature news article).","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution, so that bleaching is mapped directly"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS), which already runs the long-term monitoring program for the reef"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"One satellite is therefore used. This also simplifies communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":54.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"All major elements addressed with STK screenshots, access reports, hand calculations and file list. Checks verified: inclination, azimuth, period, aperture, decay rates (0.40 m/orbit, 2.2 km/yr; sail 5.8 m/orbit), data budget (115 Gbit → 29 Gbit vs 100 Gbit). Gaps: no X-band link budget (acknowledged); revisit estimate approximate; only one 60,000 km² strip/day is conservative.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"sin β = cos 97.79° / cos 5.2° = −0.1355 / 0.9959 = −0.136, so β = −7.8°"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"cos i = −(1.991 × 10⁻⁷) / (1.5 × 1.0826 × 10⁻³ × 0.8354 × 1.0831 × 10⁻³) = −0.1355, so i = 97.79°"},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"With a 4 m² sail the satellite re-enters about two years after the end of the mission."},{"where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings","quote":"The following STK 11 files are submitted with this report in ZYZZsat_STK.zip."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions are quantitative, drawn directly from the design sections, and include a candid limitations list (Vega C maturity, unproven fluid lensing at 5 m, ground-station equipment, 45-day mosaic). Slight improvement possible on recommendation detail.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"A 600 km sun-synchronous orbit at 97.79° inclination gives the same morning lighting on every pass, a 5 m image with a 60 km swath"},{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear structure, concise prose, well-captioned numbered figures/tables, executive summary, all references [1]-[15] cited in text. Deductions: vague references [4] and [15], Figure 2 generation method not stated, minor formatting/typographic slips (factor-of-ten expressions, unit style) and no table of figures.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report presents the design of ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), a single Earth observation microsatellite"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"Table 4: Orbital elements of ZYZZsat at the scenario epoch, 3 Feb 2025 00:00:00 UTCG (mean elements, J2000 frame)."}]}]}
